  "articleBody": "It's a common and frustrating problem: your cabin's interior sealant is starting to peel or show wear. Don't panic, but don't just put another coat on top! When sealant peels, it's usually a sign that the surface wasn't prepped correctly, the wrong product was used, or the existing coating is simply past its prime. The key to fixing this is proper preparation. You'll likely need to clean the surface thoroughly and potentially remove the old, failing sealant entirely. Trying to seal over a peeling layer will just lead to the new coat peeling off as well, creating a bigger mess down the road. You also need to make sure the new sealant is compatible with any existing coatings you decide to leave. This is where things can get a bit tricky. If you're dealing with extensive peeling, uncertain compatibility, or uneven wear, getting a professional opinion can save you a lot of headaches and ensure the new finish adheres properly and lasts. They can identify the root cause and apply a system that will protect your cabin's interior effectively.",
